This is a fork. The changes carried here are stated:

  2026-09-26  DecodePartial reports what a truncated stream did decode.

              Decode is all-or-nothing: a file still arriving yields a nil
              image and "not enough image data" whatever fraction of it is
              present, which is the same as having nothing. image/jpeg and
              image/png answer the same way, so this is a gap in the shape
              of every decoder rather than in one of them.

              DecodePartial returns the first frame at its full declared
              size together with the number of pixel rows that are FINISHED,
              counting from the top.

              readImageDescriptor already reads a frame's pixels with one
              readFull, which writes what arrived before reporting that it
              ran out -- so the rows are in the buffer and only the count
              and the frame were discarded. The change reads with
              io.ReadFull instead, because the COUNT is what was missing,
              and keeps both. An INTERLACED frame records nothing, because
              its rows arrive in passes covering the whole frame and
              uninterlace has not run; DecodePartial then refuses it.

              Only the first frame is ever reported, which costs nothing:
              decode returns as soon as one frame is whole when it is not
              keeping all of them, so a truncated animation whose first
              frame arrived decodes successfully with every row.

  2026-09-26  writer.go uses encoding/binary rather than internal/byteorder,
              which is not importable outside the standard library. Two
              calls, byte for byte the same.

              Decode, DecodeAll, DecodeConfig, Encode and EncodeAll behave
              exactly as before, and Go's own test suite passes here
              unaltered apart from the testdata paths.
